open-source license. It imposes restrictions — particularly around commercial
redistribution of source code — that conventional open-source licenses do not
impose.

"Source-available" is a descriptive category, not a single standardized
license. This document is a standalone legal instrument. Because it restricts
commercial redistribution, software under this license generally will not
qualify as "open source" under the Open Source Definition maintained by the
Open Source Initiative, and will not be OSI-approved.

This license was drafted with care but is a custom legal document. If you
intend to rely on it for a commercial product, enforce it, or need certainty
about how it applies to your situation, consult a qualified lawyer licensed
in the relevant jurisdiction before doing so.

1. Definitions

1.1 "Work" means the original software made available by the Licensor under
this License, including its source code, documentation, and associated files,
in the form distributed by the Licensor.

1.2 "Source Form" means the preferred human-readable form of the Work for
making modifications, including source code, configuration files, and
documentation source.

1.3 "Object Form" means any form resulting from mechanical transformation of
Source Form, including compiled object code, generated documentation, and
other non-source forms.

1.4 "Official Compiled Binary" means a build, executable, package, or other
Object Form release that is published by the Licensor, or explicitly
designated by the Licensor as official, through the Project's official
distribution channels (as designated under Section 1.13). A build or package
is not an Official Compiled Binary merely because it was compiled from the
Work's Source Form by a third party, regardless of accuracy or fidelity to the
original.

1.5 "Modify" / "Modification" means to alter, add to, or delete from the
Source Form of the Work, or to create a work based on the Work.

1.6 "Material Modification" means a Modification that adds, removes, or
materially alters the functionality, behavior, or purpose of the Work, judged
by substance rather than form. A Material Modification must reflect a genuine,
non-trivial change to what the software does or how it operates.

Without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the following, without more,
generally do not by themselves constitute a Material Modification:

* changes to whitespace, formatting, or code style;

* changes limited to comments or documentation;

* renaming of variables, functions, files, or the project itself;

* version number changes;

* trivial or cosmetic configuration changes that do not alter functional
  behavior;

* cosmetic-only user interface changes (e.g., color, layout, wording) that do
  not alter functionality.

This list is illustrative and non-exhaustive. Where a Modification's status
is ambiguous, it shall be evaluated according to its substance and practical
effect, not merely its form or how it is described.

1.7 "Fork" means a version of the Work, or a Derivative Work, that a person or
entity distributes to others, whether in Source Form or Object Form.

1.8 "Derivative Work" means any work, in Source Form or Object Form, based on
or derived from the Work, including a Fork.

1.9 "Contribution" means any work intentionally submitted by a person or
entity ("Contributor") to the Licensor for inclusion in the Work.

1.10 "Commercial Use" means use of the Work, in any form, in connection with
a business or for-profit activity, including internal business operations,
whether or not direct payment is made for the Work itself.

1.11 "Internal Use" means use of the Work, including a privately Modified
version, within an organization, without Commercial Distribution (as defined
below) of that Modified version to any third party.

1.12 "Commercial Distribution" means any of the following, whether done
directly or through an intermediary:

* (a) selling, licensing, or otherwise providing access to the Work's Source
  Form, or to a Modified version of its Source Form, in exchange for payment
  or other commercial consideration;

* (b) selling or commercially distributing a compiled or packaged product
  built from a Materially Modified Fork;

* (c) bundling the Work's Source Form, or a Materially Modified version of
  it, into a paid product, paid service, or paid support offering;

* (d) hosting or operating a Materially Modified version of the Work as a
  network-accessible or "software-as-a-service" offering for which users or
  customers pay, or which otherwise generates for-profit commercial benefit
  for the operator.

A distribution of the Work or a Fork is not Commercial Distribution solely
because a recipient subsequently puts it to Commercial Use, provided the
distributor did not know of, intend, authorize, or materially facilitate that
subsequent Commercial Use. For example:

* publishing a Modified Fork publicly and without cost, with no knowledge of
  or intent regarding how recipients will use it, is not Commercial
  Distribution, even if some recipient later uses it commercially;

* distributing a Fork while explicitly marketing, advertising, or structuring
  that distribution as a means for recipients to obtain the Work for Commercial
  Use without obtaining a commercial license under Section 7 is Commercial
  Distribution by the distributor, because it knowingly and materially
  facilitates circumvention of this License.

1.13 "Official Channels" means distribution channels (such as a specific
repository, release page, package registry entry, or website) that the
Licensor designates, from time to time, as official for the Work. The
Licensor may update or change its Official Channels by providing reasonable
public notice.

1.14 "Non-Commercial Redistribution" means distribution of a Fork that does
not constitute Commercial Distribution.

